391
00:31:51,734 --> 00:31:56,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That space, whatever I perceive it as, as a thought, that's not the space.

392
00:31:59,118 --> 00:31:59,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, it is.

393
00:31:59,998 --> 00:32:01,039
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all the space you have.

394
00:32:02,519 --> 00:32:03,480
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's either going to be...

395
00:32:03,800 --> 00:32:06,261
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no real space as opposed to space you have.

396
00:32:06,821 --> 00:32:10,103
[SPEAKER_00]: There's only conceived space, or space free from conceivings.

397
00:32:11,145 --> 00:32:15,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So, an assumed space, or space known for what it is, and not assumed.

398
00:32:16,509 --> 00:32:20,592
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, you're trying to have both at the same time, and compare them, and see which one's right.

399
00:32:28,358 --> 00:32:39,947
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the vision of space, it's not technically of space, it's of things, that negatively reveal, or rather, show the space as there, as not things.

400
00:32:43,744 --> 00:32:54,367
So, you're kind of saying that the difference is like, instead of saying this is in space, rather one should say there are things that disclose the presence of space.

401
00:32:54,768 --> 00:32:55,008
Yes.

402
00:32:55,668 --> 00:33:04,231
[SPEAKER_00]: And that disclosed space is more fundamental in regard to those particular things that disclose it.

403
00:33:05,051 --> 00:33:11,613
[SPEAKER_00]: But, from the point of view of the mind and the five aggregates, it isn't the ultimate fundamental.

404
00:33:13,588 --> 00:33:25,232
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, if you use space in a way to practice contemplation in regard to particular things, that's fine, because basically that space will stand for the mind.

405
00:33:26,192 --> 00:33:39,477
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to be careful not to develop views that you are somehow in this universal public space, and give it, like, and make it an entity, that it's independent of itself.

406
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:45,121
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to do that, well, then don't forget that the mind is the fundamental thing.

407
00:33:46,141 --> 00:33:59,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, what I'm saying, you can be... take particular things and then practice contemplation in regard to development of the mind, discerning of the nature of the thoughts in the mind and citta, because that already includes that space.

408
00:34:11,649 --> 00:34:17,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, all you want to do is develop, or rather discern, the principle between the actual and the peripheral.

409
00:34:19,474 --> 00:34:22,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that actual is the sense object, peripheral will be space in regard to it.

410
00:34:23,216 --> 00:34:26,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's fine, if you develop knowledge of the principle.

411
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:34,163
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start looking at that peripheral space as the actual, well, then mind will be peripheral in regard to it.

412
00:34:34,323 --> 00:34:36,584
[SPEAKER_00]: The five aggregates will be peripheral in regard to it.

413
00:34:38,385 --> 00:34:40,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you develop, again, understanding of the same principle.

414
00:34:41,484 --> 00:34:53,787
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you don't want to be discerning peripheral space in regard to things and then just assuming the same space that shares the same in regard to the mind as well.

415
00:34:55,468 --> 00:34:59,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you're not developing principle of the simultaneous present.

416
00:34:59,149 --> 00:35:01,049
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not discerning it in regard to either.

417
00:35:05,991 --> 00:35:07,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So, space is always peripheral.

418
00:35:08,031 --> 00:35:08,731
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always negative.

419
00:35:10,092 --> 00:35:10,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless when you think it.

420
00:35:13,178 --> 00:35:13,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it isn't.

421
00:35:18,363 --> 00:35:19,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can say something else is.

422
00:35:21,807 --> 00:35:25,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means what you're thinking of is then not this misconceived space.

423
00:35:25,891 --> 00:35:29,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you think of space where the space cannot be found.

424
00:35:53,065 --> 00:35:59,449
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, from the point of view of discerning the peripheral, it doesn't matter if you refer to it as space or as the mind, to the peripheral.

425
00:36:00,289 --> 00:36:04,271
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how you refer to it, as long as it's seen as peripheral.

426
00:36:11,436 --> 00:36:16,478
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, when you have a thought about space, then, you remain mindful in regard to peripheral.

427
00:36:17,099 --> 00:36:20,621
[SPEAKER_00]: You remain mindful of the peripheral in regard to that thought, this is space.

428
00:36:21,562 --> 00:36:24,184
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, soon enough, you'll see that that's not the space.

429
00:36:24,404 --> 00:36:26,145
[SPEAKER_00]: The space is always on the peripheral level.

430
00:36:27,386 --> 00:36:32,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, your experiences of space can be in the space.

431
00:36:44,297 --> 00:36:50,841
[SPEAKER_00]: In the space would mean, basically, when you would feel to give a complete independent

432
00:36:52,451 --> 00:37:02,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, complete independence to the notion of actual thought space and then attribute even the peripheral to it.

433
00:37:02,414 --> 00:37:03,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, it's in the space.

434
00:37:07,336 --> 00:37:09,317
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's a... that's a view.

435
00:37:09,397 --> 00:37:10,277
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a wrong view.

436
00:37:10,877 --> 00:37:11,998
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a wrong assumption.

437
00:37:14,639 --> 00:37:20,080
[SPEAKER_02]: Like you're saying, if I say something in space, it's assuming space was already kind of just there hanging out.

438
00:37:20,180 --> 00:37:20,661
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

439
00:37:20,881 --> 00:37:21,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

440
00:37:21,661 --> 00:37:21,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

441
00:37:26,285 --> 00:37:40,549
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can have a thought, oh, the space, in the space, but don't give it that full independence and don't attribute that which is peripheral in regard to that thought, this space.

442
00:37:41,169 --> 00:37:48,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't attribute the peripheral to it, as in everything is in the space, because that's completely gratuitous.

443
00:37:50,051 --> 00:37:51,812
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no basis for that assumption.

444
00:38:11,767 --> 00:38:21,837
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to discern space in the right terms, you know, you don't have to try to think it out, because it's pointless.

445
00:38:23,158 --> 00:38:28,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Just feel the feeling, perceive whatever is being perceived simultaneously.

446
00:38:29,064 --> 00:38:31,747
[SPEAKER_00]: That experience will be the experience of space.

447
00:38:40,747 --> 00:38:46,628
[SPEAKER_00]: And that experience even precedes the craving that you have now to fit the space in the right terms.

448
00:38:50,809 --> 00:38:56,190
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it precedes the craving, then, well, it's... it's... it's fit in the right terms, not in your terms.

449
00:38:59,030 --> 00:39:07,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, then, any notions of... that you might have had then, before that, of, oh, space was this, space is that, it just becomes pointless.

450
00:39:07,532 --> 00:39:08,752
[SPEAKER_00]: That, uh, irrelevant.

451
00:39:22,938 --> 00:39:27,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, well, that's the thing.

452
00:39:27,359 --> 00:39:30,779
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm pointing out.

453
00:39:30,879 --> 00:39:34,520
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to get out by thinking your way out.

454
00:39:35,980 --> 00:39:38,381
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to get out by seeing it for what it is.

455
00:39:38,941 --> 00:39:41,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, accept it, that you have notions of space.

456
00:39:42,461 --> 00:39:46,282
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that's a present, that is an experience as a whole for what it is.

457
00:39:47,002 --> 00:39:51,063
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, in regard to that, feel the feelings, perceive those perceptions.

458
00:39:52,902 --> 00:39:54,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, uproot it with mindfulness.

459
00:39:54,803 --> 00:39:57,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't try to figure it out and explain it to yourself.

460
00:40:02,966 --> 00:40:08,008
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it needs to be understood, and seen, and discerned, but it doesn't mean explain to yourself.

461
00:40:13,270 --> 00:40:18,332
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's kind of why I wanted to understand something, but not necessarily be able to put it into words.

462
00:40:18,873 --> 00:40:19,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

463
00:40:19,953 --> 00:40:20,153
[SPEAKER_00]: No?

464
00:40:20,373 --> 00:40:20,633
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

465
00:40:20,813 --> 00:40:22,434
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you did not quite understand it.

466
00:40:25,038 --> 00:40:30,420
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you qualify it just as I said it.

467
00:40:30,860 --> 00:40:36,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Explaining it to yourself is not the same as understanding it.

468
00:40:36,301 --> 00:40:40,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Explaining it is finding a plausible explanation from the point of view that you still have.

469
00:40:41,542 --> 00:40:42,883
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I explained it.

470
00:40:42,983 --> 00:40:44,163
[SPEAKER_00]: It's plausible to me now.

471
00:40:45,183 --> 00:40:52,785
[SPEAKER_00]: But the criteria of what's plausible or not, it's not fully developed, obviously, because otherwise you wouldn't be resorting to the temporal explanations of things.

472
00:40:54,805 --> 00:40:58,846
[SPEAKER_00]: What you're referring to is describing something once it has been understood.

473
00:40:59,826 --> 00:41:01,447
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's definitely not an explanation.

474
00:41:01,787 --> 00:41:02,487
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a description.

475
00:41:02,867 --> 00:41:03,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Because why?

476
00:41:03,468 --> 00:41:07,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for throughout the description, the thing remains present because it's understood.

477
00:41:08,329 --> 00:41:09,649
[SPEAKER_00]: In explanation, that's not the case.

478
00:41:09,849 --> 00:41:11,530
[SPEAKER_01]: In explanation, you might arrive at it.

479
00:41:11,870 --> 00:41:16,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, so you think you arrived at it when you explained it.

480
00:41:17,692 --> 00:41:19,892
[SPEAKER_00]: But that means you didn't have it to begin with.

481
00:41:20,092 --> 00:41:22,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, how can you explain it if you don't have it to begin with?

482
00:41:24,054 --> 00:41:26,535
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, you want to replace explanations with descriptions.

483
00:41:27,716 --> 00:41:28,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Phenological descriptions.

484
00:41:29,156 --> 00:41:29,957
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what suttas are.

485
00:41:30,497 --> 00:41:31,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Prescure descriptions.

486
00:41:51,706 --> 00:41:52,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Nalu, second.

487
00:41:56,518 --> 00:41:56,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Peripheral.

488
00:41:58,419 --> 00:42:01,800
[SPEAKER_00]: See, even the notion of peripheral is already a notion of expanse.

489
00:42:01,820 --> 00:42:02,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Place as well.

490
00:42:02,621 --> 00:42:02,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Place.

491
00:42:03,201 --> 00:42:04,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in a way, yeah.

492
00:42:04,722 --> 00:42:05,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, place.

493
00:42:06,082 --> 00:42:06,662
[SPEAKER_00]: The expanse.

494
00:42:08,823 --> 00:42:14,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is actual, and then the... everything else that's not there, it's already the expanse in regard to it.

495
00:42:14,846 --> 00:42:15,726
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the mind is.

496
00:42:16,547 --> 00:42:17,447
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the space is.

497
00:42:19,328 --> 00:42:21,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Like foreground and background.

498
00:42:21,669 --> 00:42:22,610
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fairly the same thing.

499
00:42:22,770 --> 00:42:23,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, yeah.

500
00:42:24,445 --> 00:42:31,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why... that's why you don't... that's what I mean, like, when you have... even if you have a thought of doubt, what is space then?

501
00:42:32,529 --> 00:42:38,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Find peripheral in regard to that, and that's where the space is.

502
00:42:42,174 --> 00:42:45,856
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how it becomes impenetrable to doubt as well, because doubt can only be actual.

503
00:43:00,920 --> 00:43:13,243
[SPEAKER_02]: Going back a bit, that whole thing we've just been talking about is... is the reason why we can also do... designate things like, oh, internal, external, that's also spatial.

504
00:43:13,623 --> 00:43:14,243
Yes, yeah.

505
00:43:14,563 --> 00:43:20,724
Basically, then, external would kind of naturally be attributed to anything that's sort of...

506
00:43:20,784 --> 00:43:21,364
In space.

507
00:43:21,924 --> 00:43:24,065
In space, actual, through the senses.

508
00:43:24,865 --> 00:43:29,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else, this whole, the expanse I was referring to, would be kind of this ambiguous internal.

509
00:43:31,582 --> 00:43:48,293
[SPEAKER_00]: But when that's also seen as simultaneously present phenomenon, not as the sense object, obviously, but as that which pertains to the mind, then, yeah, either way, whether it's internal or external, are seen basically as external in regard to you, to your point of view.

510
00:43:50,875 --> 00:44:00,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, for the untrained mind, things around me that I can see, touch, taste, smell, taste, so on, those are the ones that I can access, that I can have.

511
00:44:00,883 --> 00:44:02,164
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's everything is external.

512
00:44:03,806 --> 00:44:09,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything that's not that is internal and I'm kind of in between and everywhere.

513
00:44:11,813 --> 00:44:17,158
[SPEAKER_00]: And usually, people are that, for them, their own thoughts are not even seen as something that exists.

514
00:44:18,579 --> 00:44:23,063
[SPEAKER_00]: They clearly talk about what they think, but they will not regard it, they will not give it the same

515
00:44:24,646 --> 00:44:29,128
[SPEAKER_00]: amount of existence as they would to the things that they can obtain through the senses.

516
00:44:29,308 --> 00:44:29,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Why the mind?

517
00:44:29,888 --> 00:44:42,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind got so far out, proliferated towards the senses, that that became the threshold of what's real, what the being is, what the what the extent of the being is.

518
00:44:42,894 --> 00:44:46,435
[SPEAKER_00]: And then everything under that threshold is just assumed to not exist.

519
00:44:46,455 --> 00:44:52,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Although that's a contradiction in terms of

520
00:44:54,145 --> 00:44:55,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can say this doesn't exist.

521
00:44:56,666 --> 00:44:58,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it clearly exists, because otherwise you couldn't refer to it.

522
00:45:03,971 --> 00:45:09,994
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you attribute non-existence to something that doesn't appear at all?

523
00:45:11,215 --> 00:45:11,695
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

524
00:45:11,955 --> 00:45:16,358
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you attributed non-existence to something means it has appeared, which means it actually exists.

525
00:45:29,228 --> 00:45:31,248
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the same with mindfulness.

526
00:45:31,268 --> 00:45:34,789
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it is, when it arises.

527
00:45:35,949 --> 00:45:36,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

528
00:45:36,730 --> 00:45:36,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

529
00:45:37,670 --> 00:45:38,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

530
00:45:38,170 --> 00:45:38,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

531
00:45:38,370 --> 00:45:39,430
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that's what that's what I mean.

532
00:45:39,450 --> 00:45:51,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't... Every reflection, every contemplation, having mentioned that in the last talk that I had with you, has to be rooted in the experience as a whole.

533
00:45:52,313 --> 00:45:53,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the experience as a whole.

534
00:45:55,853 --> 00:45:56,874
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be rooted there.

535
00:45:56,994 --> 00:45:58,274
[SPEAKER_00]: That has to be the starting point.

536
00:45:59,633 --> 00:46:01,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about space on the basis of that.

537
00:46:02,134 --> 00:46:05,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on the basis of your preconceived notion of the space that you had from before.

538
00:46:10,696 --> 00:46:15,997
[SPEAKER_00]: You actually have to... You might feel the craving to, no, no, no, but I have to understand it on these terms.

539
00:46:16,017 --> 00:46:16,418
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

540
00:46:17,458 --> 00:46:28,661
[SPEAKER_00]: It's to say no to those terms, and re-establish the terms on the basis of the mindfulness of the experience as a whole, and then there, think about space, without losing that basis of that mindfulness.

541
00:46:32,765 --> 00:46:34,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, then you discern space within that.

542
00:46:35,226 --> 00:46:35,966
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't assume it.

543
00:46:36,066 --> 00:46:36,927
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't fabricate it.

544
00:46:37,927 --> 00:46:39,848
[SPEAKER_00]: You find it for what it is.

545
00:46:47,390 --> 00:46:53,933
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your path.

546
00:46:53,953 --> 00:46:55,694
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point of view that makes everything else.

547
00:46:56,474 --> 00:46:58,074
[SPEAKER_01]: It reveals everything else.

548
00:46:58,094 --> 00:46:59,415
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the eight points.

549
00:47:02,057 --> 00:47:02,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, pretty much.

550
00:47:02,517 --> 00:47:04,199
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, as I said, you...

551
00:47:04,259 --> 00:47:10,384
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm pretty sure I mentioned it in that last thought as well.

552
00:47:10,424 --> 00:47:16,348
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to know what you're supposed to do with that view.

553
00:47:32,013 --> 00:47:33,854
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to know that that view is not the end.

554
00:47:34,775 --> 00:47:35,595
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just the beginning.

555
00:47:36,956 --> 00:47:40,838
[SPEAKER_00]: That view, basically, is the view upon which you can develop discernment.

556
00:47:40,858 --> 00:47:42,379
[SPEAKER_00]: That view is not discernment.

557
00:47:44,080 --> 00:47:44,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

558
00:47:44,981 --> 00:47:45,921
[SPEAKER_00]: The lens.

559
00:47:46,161 --> 00:47:46,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

560
00:47:46,902 --> 00:47:47,682
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a tool.

561
00:47:48,683 --> 00:47:52,385
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a tool, exactly, for seeing things through that lens.

562
00:47:53,215 --> 00:48:02,680
[SPEAKER_00]: But for most people, mindfulness of experience is always the ultimate kind of culmination of the spiritual practice, whereby you just stay mindful in this pure, pristine awareness and whatnot.

563
00:48:04,361 --> 00:48:07,803
[SPEAKER_00]: You just stay mindful in your ignorance, because you're clearly not unraveling it.

564
00:48:09,964 --> 00:48:11,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no, too much thinking.

565
00:48:11,084 --> 00:48:11,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

566
00:48:11,664 --> 00:48:18,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Too much thinking, if you're thinking on a non-mindful base, then, yeah, you're just revolving around the same

567
00:48:19,168 --> 00:48:26,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Same ignorance, same assumptions, and basically psychotherapeutizing your way out of it.

568
00:48:27,515 --> 00:48:30,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Explaining it to yourself, managing whatever else.

569
00:48:30,577 --> 00:48:31,438
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the dhamma.

570
00:48:31,458 --> 00:48:32,319
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the practice.

571
00:48:34,721 --> 00:48:41,466
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you establish the mindful base, or the mindfulness of the experience as a whole, then you can be all the thinking you want.

572
00:48:45,349 --> 00:48:46,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, it's the right mindful thinking.

573
00:48:47,050 --> 00:48:49,612
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the thinking, basically, it's the dhamma.

574
00:48:51,453 --> 00:48:54,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Another thought for mental object is dhammas.

575
00:49:05,643 --> 00:49:06,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

576
00:49:06,964 --> 00:49:11,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But people either dismiss the thinking, as just thinking, well,

577
00:49:12,670 --> 00:49:16,813
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you know you're supposed to look for within that.

578
00:49:16,993 --> 00:49:21,236
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know that by reading the sutras, by discerning the sutras, by investigating what they mean.

579
00:49:45,468 --> 00:49:48,451
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't tell you the answers by itself, which is what people think.

